R-25b.6 — the dev-droplet cloud firewall

Visibility tested first, as the ruling requires:
  yallo-web-dev   473723796  178.62.82.59  lon1  active   <- in this team
  yallo-web-prod  473723897  138.68.140.42 lon1  active   <- NOT touched

BEFORE (no firewall existed):
  http://178.62.82.59/    308   reachable directly, bypassing Cloudflare
  https://dev.yallo.co/   301   through Cloudflare

Firewall fdc70367-c3a3-4f2f-95f4-0c0532cb2856 'yallo-web-dev-cloudflare-only',
attached to 473723796 only. 22 Cloudflare CIDRs (15 v4 + 7 v6), fetched from
cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and ips-v6 at the time of creation.

RULE SET, read back from the API:
  name: yallo-web-dev-cloudflare-only | status: succeeded | droplets: [473723796]
  IN  icmp  ports=0     sources= 2 OPEN TO ALL
  IN  tcp   ports=22    sources= 2 OPEN TO ALL
  IN  tcp   ports=80    sources=22 Cloudflare only
  IN  tcp   ports=443   sources=22 Cloudflare only
  OUT icmp  ports=0    
  OUT tcp   ports=0    
  OUT udp   ports=0    

PORT 22 IS RESTATED, NOT OMITTED. A DigitalOcean cloud firewall is
default-deny inbound once attached, so leaving 22 out of the rule set would
have CLOSED it. The ruling says untouched, so its current reachability is
restated exactly. Same reasoning for outbound, which is allowed in full.

AFTER, measured from this machine (a non-Cloudflare address):
  http://178.62.82.59/    no answer   (was 308)
  https://178.62.82.59/   no answer
  https://dev.yallo.co/   301 -> https://yallo.co/   still works through Cloudflare
  tcp/22                  reachable, unchanged

Reversible by detaching: doctl compute firewall delete fdc70367-c3a3-4f2f-95f4-0c0532cb2856
